<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Torrent on Madhav Khosla</title><link>https://madhavkhoslaa.github.io/tags/torrent/</link><description>Recent content in Torrent on Madhav Khosla</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><copyright>© Madhav Khosla</copyright><lastBuildDate>Thu, 09 Nov 2023 02:01:58 +0530</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://madhavkhoslaa.github.io/tags/torrent/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Torrent: 1. How a .torrent file works</title><link>https://madhavkhoslaa.github.io/draft/torrent_file/</link><pubDate>Thu, 09 Nov 2023 02:01:58 +0530</pubDate><guid>https://madhavkhoslaa.github.io/draft/torrent_file/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;img src="https://madhavkhoslaa.github.io/images/NQFCFTC.jpeg" alt="image"&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 class="heading" id="scope-of-this-blog"&gt;&#10; Scope of this blog&lt;span class="heading__anchor"&gt; &lt;a href="#scope-of-this-blog"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;To be able to understand what a torrent file does. What does contents inside torrent file do.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 class="heading" id="how-you-download-a-torrent"&gt;&#10; How you download a torrent.&lt;span class="heading__anchor"&gt; &lt;a href="#how-you-download-a-torrent"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;Say you were to download arch linux from the provided torrent file on the archlinux website. You would&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;ol&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;Download the file from the website&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;li&gt;load the file onto a torrent client like deluge/transmission/uTorrent etc and it begins downloading.&lt;/li&gt;&#10;&lt;/ol&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 class="heading" id="observing-a-torrent-file"&gt;&#10; Observing a torrent file&lt;span class="heading__anchor"&gt; &lt;a href="#observing-a-torrent-file"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;Let&amp;rsquo;s disect the &lt;a href="https://archlinux.org/releng/releases/2023.11.01/torrent/"&gt;Arch Linux Iso&lt;/a&gt; and open the file.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;The file looks alot gibberish but you can see some ascii charecters like d,i,i with numbers next to them and maybe some urls.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;this encoding of a file is called bencoding and it is a representation of a object similar to how yaml/json works.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;now that we know that it is bencoded, we should know how bencoding works.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;h1 class="heading" id="working-of-bencoding"&gt;&#10; Working of bencoding&lt;span class="heading__anchor"&gt; &lt;a href="#working-of-bencoding"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;h1 class="heading" id="opening-torrent-file-in-a-bencoding-viewer-tool"&gt;&#10; Opening torrent file in a bencoding viewer tool&lt;span class="heading__anchor"&gt; &lt;a href="#opening-torrent-file-in-a-bencoding-viewer-tool"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&#10;&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;this &lt;a href="https://chocobo1.github.io/bencode_online/"&gt;tool&lt;/a&gt; helps us open a bencoded file and let&amp;rsquo;s see it in JSON format.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Let&amp;rsquo;s see the file in JSON which is more human readable format for an object.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div class="chroma"&gt;&#10;&lt;table class="lntable"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td class="lntd"&gt;&#10;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 1&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 2&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 3&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 4&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 5&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 6&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 7&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 8&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt; 9&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;10&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;11&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt"&gt;12&#10;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="lnt